# Database migrations

Connection setup and direct vs pooled URLs: [apps/api/migrations/README.md](../../apps/api/migrations/README.md). Render does not run migrations and deploys the API when `apps/api/**` reaches `main`: [docs/deployment.md](../../docs/deployment.md).

## Approval

Show the SQL and wait. Do not run `pnpm db:up`, `pnpm db:down`, `pnpm db:create-user`, or any other SQL on Neon until the user has seen that SQL and approved it. `pnpm db:create` only writes a file. `pnpm db:status` does not apply migrations, but once SQL files exist Goose may create its version table even for status.

## Shape of a migration

- Create it with `pnpm db:create <name>` from the repo root. Keep the generated timestamp. It must sort after every migration already in the branch, including one on a parent PR you stacked on.
- Do not edit a migration that has already been applied on Neon. Add another file.
- Every file has a `-- +goose Down` that reverses Up: drop only what Up added, in reverse dependency order.
- Keep Up additive and safe while the current API is still serving. New tables, or new columns that are nullable or `NOT NULL` with a default, are the expected shape. Do not rewrite or delete existing content rows to change the schema.
- Create accounts with `pnpm db:create-user`, never inside a migration.

## Apply before the API reads the new shape

If the API in a PR reads tables or columns the migration adds, run `pnpm db:up` on production Neon before merging. Merging `apps/api/**` to `main` deploys that API to Render immediately, and Render will not migrate. Shipping the queries first takes the library down.

Use the same order locally. Do not restart `pnpm dev:api` on code that reads the new columns until `db:up` has run. Restarting early already broke the library once (`cover_key`); the rows were fine and production was untouched. Tell the user not to restart early.

A migration that exists only on a pull request is not on production. Say that in the PR. Do not describe it as applied.

## Proving the SQL

Fake-store tests do not run SQL. After approval, exercise the change against Neon with a temporary API on another port (for example 8099). Do not point the user's running dev API at the new queries first.

Local and production share one Neon database, so they share the R2 bucket `infu-media-prod`. Split a Neon branch and a bucket together, or not at all. See architecture and deployment.
